\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 To what extent was the empire important for Britain\rquote s trade and commerce, 1857\endash 1967?\par
\par
Date: 1857\par
Main economic issues: Free trade the dominant theory \endash  Britain the foremost trading nation in the world. Aggressive promotion of free trade through 'gunboat diplomacy' (see Opium Wars with China).\par
It was important: Significant imports came from the colonies \endash  control guaranteed security of supply (e.g. cotton from India; cocoa from Gold Coast etc.). Colonial economies were reliable markets for export of British goods (e.g. clothing to India). Control of key colonial possessions around the world was important to guarantee Britain\rquote s naval hegemony, thus guaranteeing trade routes (e.g. Gibraltar, Cape Town, Hong Kong, Singapore etc.).\par
It wasn\rquote t important: Europe and the USA accounted for 2/3 of Britain\rquote s \f1\'a350 million exports. The USA was Britain\rquote s greatest single trading partner (esp. export of cotton). British West Indies were a commercial backwater \f0\endash  sugar imported more cheaply from Brazil and Cuba rather than Jamaica. Informal empire preferred over direct control.\par
\par
Date: 1860\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1867: Britain\rquote s exports to non-imperial markets were \f1\'a3131 million out of \'a3181 million total (especially expansion in South American markets worth \'a312 million).\par
\par
Date: 1870\par
Main economic issues: 1873\f0\endash 1890s: \lquote Great Depression\rquote  \endash  slump in world trade \f2\u8594?\f1  protectionism introduced by Germany and other powers. The Scramble for Africa takes place, in part to secure raw materials and markets.\par
It was important: One of the reasons for colonial expansion in Africa in the later 19th century was clearly economic \f0\endash  acquiring possession of raw materials and guaranteeing markets: security of investment in Egypt and the Suez Canal leads to occupation in 1882; economic motives clearly important in South Africa \endash  diamonds and gold; other colonies in Africa: palm oil in Nigeria; cocoa in Gold Coast; groundnuts in Gambia etc.\par
It wasn\rquote t important: Reasons other than economic are also clearly important in explaining expansion in Africa in the later 19th century (Nyasaland, British Somaliland and the Sudan had little economic value). Historiography: Gallagher and Robinson (Africa and the Victorians, 1961) downplay the role of economics in explaining empire and emphasise Britain\rquote s responses to crises on the periphery to secure their strategic position.\par
\par
Date: 1880\par
Main economic issues: Growing fear of British \lquote decline\rquote  relative to other powers: British share of world trade slumps from 23% in 1880 to 17% in 1910.\par
It was important: An example of the importance of direct control to British trade is Egypt where, by 1880, 76% of all exports were cotton to Britain (93% by 1912).\par
It wasn\rquote t important: In the 1890s Britain still did more trade with Belgium than it did with the whole of sub-Saharan Africa.\par
\par
Date: 1890\par
Main economic issues: Debate over free trade vs. Imperial Protection \endash  the latter promoted by Joseph Chamberlain \endash  ideas defeated in 1906 election.\par
It was important: 1899 Colonial Loans Act - \f1\'a33 million in grants for colonial development to encourage economic growth and trade with Britain.\par
\par
Date: 1900\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1909 Trade \f0\endash  Imports from sub-Saharan Africa: \f1\'a312.3 million; Imports from Europe: \'a3241.6 million; Exports to sub-Saharan Africa: \'a325.8 million; Exports to Europe: \'a3119 million.\par
\par
Date: 1910\par
Main economic issues: British share of global industrial capacity no longer biggest: 15% compared to 16% (Germany) and 35% (USA). Costs of empire and defence (naval race with Germany) also increasing. However, Britain\rquote s overseas investment was continuing to grow (doubles from \'a32 billion in 1900 to \'a34 billion in 1913), as was its share in \lquote invisibles\rquote  which balances the picture.\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1913 \f0\endash  62.8% of exports and 75.1% of imports to/from non-imperial markets (imperial markets only 37.2% of exports and 24.9% of imports).\par
\par
Date: 1914\par
It was important: Imperial Preference \endash  the argument that tariff reform would make the empire self-sufficient, encourage inter-empire trade and draw the empire closer together politically \endash  these ideas, though, are defeated.\par
It wasn\rquote t important: \\$4 billion borrowed from the USA to pay for the war effort.\par
\par
Date: 1918\par
It was important: Imperial contribution to the war effort \endash  India contributes \f1\'a3100 million; dominion governments pay for their own armies and munitions. Imperial preference introduced during the war.\par
\par
Date: 1920\par
Main economic issues: End of WWI \f0\endash  huge economic costs (\f1\'a335 billion); exports damaged + sale of overseas investments to pay for the war.\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1920 \f0\endash  abandonment of wartime imperial preference and return to principles of free trade.\par
\par
Date: 1930\par
Main economic issues: 1920s \endash  decline in agricultural prices damaging colonies\rquote  terms of trade. 1929 \endash  The Wall Street Crash and the Great Depression begins \f2\u8594?\f1  by 1931 \'bd the national budget was going on welfare payments. Collapse of international trade, Britain forced off the gold standard and very high unemployment.\par
It was important: 1929 \f0\endash  Colonial Development Act encouraging investment in tropical colonies. 1932 \endash  Ottawa Conference introduces imperial preference amongst Britain and the dominions, and a 10% tariff on imports from outside the empire. Creation of the Sterling Area ensures that imperial currencies are tied together. By 1939 exports to imperial markets had risen to 49% (from 36% in 1910) and imports to 39.5% (from 25% in 1910).\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1926 \endash  of 2,400 lorries bought in the Gold Coast, only 139 were made in Britain. 1938 \endash  93% of all cotton goods sold in East Africa came from Japan. Strategic costs of imperial overstretch \endash  e.g. \f1\'a325 million spent on Singapore naval base.\par
\par
Date: 1940\par
Main economic issues: WWII \f0\endash  disruption of trade and shipping (54% of merchant fleet lost); 1/3 of Britain\rquote s overseas assets sold off; massive trade deficit and dependence on US loans and Lend-Lease.\par
It was important: Colonial Development and Welfare Acts of 1940 and 1945 to invest in colonies and develop their economies to encourage Britain\rquote s economic recovery \endash  overseen by Colonial Development Corporation, 1948. Some productive colonies such as Gold Coast (cocoa) and Malaya (rubber, tin) very important in gaining dollars for the Sterling Area (i.e. for Britain).\par
It wasn\rquote t important: During WWII Britain reliant on American Lend-Lease programme \endash  dependency on the USA.\par
\par
Date: 1945\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1946 \endash  US loan of \f1\'a33.75 billion. Britain also the largest recipient of Marshall Aid \f0\endash  dependency on the USA rather than the empire. Growing costs of imperial commitments: \f1\'a340 million cost of garrison in Palestine in 1948. Tanganyika Groundnuts Scheme (1947\f0\endash 1951) wasted \f1\'a349 million and was entirely unsuccessful.\par
\par
Date: 1950\par
It wasn\rquote t important: Greater budget strain in the 1950s \f0\endash  focus on domestic priorities (e.g. welfare state, NHS) and Cold War defence \f2\u8594?\f1  nuclear weapons and a large standing army in Europe \f2\u8594?\f1  imperial commitments an unwelcome burden.\par
\par
Date: 1960\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1965 \f0\endash  Britain increasingly trading with Western Europe rather than the empire: 29.8% imports from Commonwealth (down from 44.9% in 1948) compared to 30.6% from Europe (up from 20.5% in 1948); 27.9% exports to Commonwealth (down from 46.1% in 1948) compared to 32.5% exports to Europe (up from 24.8% in 1948).\par
\par
Date: 1967\par
Main economic issues: Sterling Crisis leading to devaluation and huge defence cuts \f2\u8594?\f1  decision to withdraw all forces from east of Suez.\par
It wasn\rquote t important: 1970s \f0\endash  abandonment of Sterling Area.\par
\par
Date: 1973\par
It wasn\rquote t important: Britain joins the EEC \endash  common European tariffs introduced which damages trade with dominions (e.g. Australia and New Zealand).\par
\par
